WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.899
Linux file security is built around ownership and permissions.

2
00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:12.564
A file has an owner, a group, and a file mode that describes who can read, write, or execute it.

3
00:00:12.564 --> 00:00:19.496
That model is simple enough to learn, but important enough to affect almost every Linux system.

4
00:00:19.496 --> 00:00:27.295
Incorrect permissions can expose sensitive data, allow unauthorized changes, prevent applications from working, or make troubleshooting much harder.

5
00:00:27.295 --> 00:00:36.826
Read access matters because many files contain useful or sensitive information: configuration, credentials, logs, application data, scripts, reports, keys, and system details.

6
00:00:36.826 --> 00:00:43.758
Write access matters because changing the wrong file can alter behavior, damage data, or weaken controls.

7
00:00:43.758 --> 00:00:49.824
Execute access matters because scripts and programs should run only where that makes sense.

8
00:00:49.824 --> 00:00:59.355
A file that is readable or writable by too many users may be a security problem even if the system appears healthy.

9
00:00:59.355 --> 00:01:01.088
Directories need special attention.

10
00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:09.753
Directory permissions affect whether users can list names, traverse paths, create files, remove files, or reach objects below the directory.

11
00:01:09.753 --> 00:01:16.252
A sensitive file can still be exposed if the path around it is poorly controlled.

12
00:01:16.252 --> 00:01:24.917
Application directories, shared folders, log locations, temporary spaces, and deployment paths should be reviewed with their real purpose in mind.

13
00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:28.816
The defensive habit is to match permissions to need.

14
00:01:28.816 --> 00:01:31.849
Sensitive files should not be broadly accessible.

15
00:01:31.849 --> 00:01:36.182
Scripts should be controlled by the right owner and group.

16
00:01:36.182 --> 00:01:40.947
Logs should be available for operations and investigation without unnecessary exposure.

17
00:01:40.947 --> 00:01:50.479
Before changing permissions on a production system, administrators should understand the application, test safely, document the change, and keep a rollback plan.
